ingle. F2 is a single. \n\nBut the user's valid actions include Play F4, which is two in hand. If I discard F4, others might need it for a pung. But since there are four remaining, maybe it's safe. Alternatively, discarding F2, which is a single. \n\nWait, the user's hand includes F2:1. The game history shows that the player across played F2 earlier. So maybe they have a F2 already. If I discard F2, they might not need it. \n\nAlternatively, discarding W7, which is a single. If I discard W7, it's a tile that's not part of any possible meld. \n\nBetween these options, which is better? \n\nIf I discard W7, I remove a tile that can't contribute to my hand. If I discard T6, same thing. \n\nLooking at the unshown tiles, W7 has 4 remaining. So discarding it would leave others with 3. Not too bad. \n\nAlternatively, if I discard B5, which is a middle tile. If someone has B4 and B6, they can form a chow. But I don't have B6. \n\nHmm. Maybe the best choice is to discard a tile that's not useful for my hand. Since my hand has two W1s, keeping one for a possible pair might be better. So discarding one of the W1s? But the valid actions include Play W1. Wait, the user's hand has W1:2, so they can play a W1. But discarding a W1 would leave one in hand. However, if I discard a W1, others might need it for a pung. \n\nBut the user's melds are empty, so they are aiming for a fully concealed hand (4 points). If they discard a W1, they might be giving up a chance to form a pung. But since they only have two W1s, they need one more to make a pung. So maybe it's better to keep both W1s for the possibility of a pung or pair. \n\nSo perhaps the best tile to discard is one that's not part of any possible meld. The T6 is a single, not part of any chow or pung. Discarding T6 would be safe. Alternatively, F2 is a single. \n\nLooking at the unshown tiles, T6 has 4 remaining. So discarding it is safe. F2 has 2 remaining. \n\nBut the player across played F2 earlier, so maybe they have one. Discarding F2 might help them form a pair. \n\nBetween T6 and W7, both are singles. Which one is better to discard? \n\nThe T6 is part of the Dots suit. The W7 is part of the Characters suit. Maybe discarding T6 is better because it's a middle tile, less likely to be needed for a chow. \n\nAlternatively, if I discard W7, it's a terminal (no, W7 is a middle tile in the Characters suit). Wait, W7 is a middle tile (Characters 7), not a terminal. Terminals are 1 and 9. So W7 is a middle tile. \n\nHmm. Maybe the best choice is to discard a tile that's part of a suit that has other tiles in the hand. For example, B1 is part of the Dots suit. If I discard B1, maybe others can use it for a chow. But I have two B1s. Discarding one leaves one. \n\nAlternatively, discarding B9, which is a terminal. Terminals are often part of pairs or chows. \n\nWait, the user's hand has B9 as a single. Discarding B9 might be safe. \n\nBut the user's valid actions include B9. Let's see the unshown tiles: B9 has 4 remaining. So discarding it is safe. \n\nBut how does this affect the hand? If I discard B9, then I have one B1, two B4s, one B5. Maybe the B4 and B5 can form part of a B4-B5-B6 chow if I draw B6. \n\nBut I don't have B6. Hmm. \n\nAlternatively, if I discard B5, then I have B4s. Maybe B4 can form a pair. \n\nThis is getting complicated. Maybe the best approach is to discard a tile that is not part of any possible meld and has a higher count remaining. \n\nLooking at the valid actions, the options include Play W7, which is a single. Discarding W7 would remove a tile that can't contribute to any meld. Similarly, T6. \n\nBetween them, which is better? \n\nThe W7 is in the Characters suit, which the user has two W1s. Maybe keeping the W1s for a pair or pung is better. So discarding W7 is better. \n\nSo the answer would be Play W7.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The best move is to discard W7 as it is a single tile not contributing to any potential melds and has a high remaining count, minimizing t