ng of West Wind (F3) might give me a Seat Wind bonus (2 points). But I only have one F3 in my hand. If I Peng B1, I still have F2, F3, F4. But no Pungs there. \n\nLooking at the other tiles, T6 is a middle tile, B4 and B5 could form part of a Chow or Pung. B9 is a terminal. The F2, F3, F4 could be part of a mixed set. But without more melds, it's unclear. \n\nIf I Peng B1, I would have a Pung of B1, which is a Pung (2 points). But since there are three B1 left in the wall, maybe it's not urgent to Peng. Also, by Penging, I might be giving up the chance to form a concealed Pung later, which is worth more points. A concealed Pung is worth 4 points, whereas an exposed Pung is 2 points. \n\nAlternatively, if I keep the B1 as concealed and wait to form a concealed Pung later, I might get more points. But if another B1 is discarded, I can't Peng it if I want to keep it concealed. However, since the previous player already discarded a B1, and there are three B1 left, maybe there's a chance to get another B1 later. \n\nBut in the current situation, the valid action is Peng B1 or Pass. So the question is, is it better to Peng now or not. If I Peng, I get a Pung, but it's exposed. If I Pass, I keep the B1 concealed, hoping to form a concealed Pung later. \n\nAlso, considering the other players' melds. The next player has a triple J3 (White Dragon), the player across has a Chi T4 (so they have T3, T4, T5). The previous player has no melds. \n\nAnother consideration is the shape of my hand. If I Peng B1, I have a Pung of B1, and the remaining tiles are T6, B4 (two), B5, B9, F2, F3, F4. That's 11 tiles. Wait, my total hand is 14 tiles (since I have 9 tiles listed, but with two copies of W1, B1, B4, and two F4). Wait, the hand tiles are listed as [W1:2; T6:1; B1:2; B4:2; B5:1; B9:1; F2:1; F3:1; F4:2]. So total tiles: 2+1+2+2+1+1+1+1+2 = 13. Wait, but a winning hand is 14 tiles. Wait, maybe the hand tiles are the current state, and the melds are empty. So I have 13 tiles in hand, 