't have B3 or B5. Hmm.\n\nWait, the other players have some melds. The player sitting across has a meld of T3, T4, T5, and another of W2, W2, W2. The next player has a pung of J3. So if I discard T4, which is part of a chow they might need, but they already have a T4 in their hand (they played T4 in their history). Maybe not critical. But I need to check if discarding certain tiles would be dangerous.\n\nLet me think about my hand's potential. If I can form all chows and a pair, that's All Chows (2 points). Or maybe some other patterns. Let's see:\n\nPossible pairs: W1, B1, B4. Let's assume I take one of these as the pair.\n\nIf I take B1 as the pair (two of them), then I need four melds. Let's see:\n\n- W1, W1, W6, W8: Not helpful for chows unless I can form sequences. W1, W2, W3? I don't have W2 or W3. W6, W7, W8? I have W6 and W8, but no W7. Similarly for others.\n\nB1 (pair), then maybe B4 could be part of a chow with B3, B4, B5. But I don't have those. Alternatively, B1, B1, B4 could be a pung? No, pung needs three of the same. So maybe not.\n\nWhat about the F1, F3, F4? Those are honor tiles. If I can form a pung or kong of winds, but I only have one of each. Not helpful.\n\nLooking at the valid actions, which tiles can I discard safely? Let's check each option:\n\n- Play F4: I have one F4. Discarding it might not be bad if others don't need it. The next player has F4 in their history (they played F4), so maybe they don't need it. The player across hasn't played F4, but I don't know. F4 is North Wind. If others have melds or need it, but not sure.\n\n- Play B1: I have two B1. If I discard one, I still have one left. If others are waiting for B1, but looking at the unshown tiles, B1 has 2 remaining. So maybe it's safe? But if someone has a meld needing B1, like a chow with B1, B2, B3. The player across has a T3, T4, T5, so maybe not. But they might have other needs.\n\n- Play B9: I have one B9. The unshown B9 has 2 left. If others have a chow needing B9, like T